Singapore's tourism industry has rebounded strongly, with inbound tourists increasing 19 times compared to 2021.
(Combined with news on the 11th) Singapore Tourism Board recently announced that it is expected that the number of international tourists visiting Singapore in 2023 will reach 12 million to 14 million, bringing about tourism revenue of about 18 billion to 21 billion new yuan; the number of tourists and tourism revenue will recover to about 67% to 75% of the level before the COVID-19 pandemic in 2019; and there is a great possibility that the tourism industry will fully recover in 2024.
Singapore's tourism market is gradually restarting and recovering in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board show that incoming tourists will increase 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million in 2022, exceeding the Board’s earlier forecast of 4 to 6 million, and that tourists have recovered to 33 percent of their pre-epidemic levels.
From January to September 2022, Singapore's tourism revenue totaled 8.96 billion Singapore dollars. It is expected that the tourism revenue in 2022 will reach 13.8 billion to 14.3 billion Singapore dollars, which is 6 to 7 times higher than 1.89 billion Singapore dollars in 2021, and has recovered to about 50% to 52% of the level before the COVID-19 pandemic in 2019.
The Singapore government is taking multiple measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism industry.
In April 2022, the government announced an allocation of nearly 500 million Singapore dollars to stimulate the recovery of the tourism industry.
The Tourism Board recently announced that it plans to allocate NZ$110 million from this budget over the next two years to launch more commercial and leisure activities to attract tourists to return.
To make health and wellness one of Singapore's tourism highlights, the Singapore Tourism Board launched an expression of interest on November 23, 2022, to develop a health and wellness attraction on the southern coast of Singapore.
Singapore Tourism Board's Executive Director of Sports and Wellness Tourism, Wang Lingli, said that in recent years, more and more tourists have put their physical health in the first place in all considerations.
Developing a health and wellness attraction in the southern waterfront area, which includes Gardens by the Bay and the Marina Barrage, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism and economy, and allow tourists to spend more time and money in Singapore.
The Director of the Singapore Tourism Authority, Chen Jianlong, said that health and wellness would become an important part of Singapore &apos; s tourism product in the coming years.
The COVID-19 pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
To this end, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and wellness programs to the local public and international tourists over the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board held the first Health and Wellness Festival, offering the public more than 130 health activities and experiences.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences for tourists.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children &apos; s Museum, the Avatar World at the Marina Bay Gardens, the Central Beach Marketplace at the Santa Monica Beach, the new &quot; Explore New Frontiers &quot; exhibition hall at the Museum of Art and Science and the new open-air theatre at the Night Wildlife Park, the Tourism Board will support the development and renewal of projects such as the Bird Park at the World of Birds, which will make Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
According to the plan, Singapore will develop industry standards for sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions and incentive tourism in 2023, with particular focus on waste management and carbon emissions reduction issues, further optimizing the travel environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
As of December 18, 2022, data shows that Changi Airport has a strong recovery in passenger flow, with more than 150,000 passengers received daily, about 1 million passengers received weekly, which is about 75% of the pre-pandemic level. The number of airline employees on duty has also recovered to about 90% of the pre-pandemic level.
Data shows that from January to October 2022, Singapore has received 23.6 million tourists, which is equivalent to 42% of the pre-pandemic period, and the take-off and landing flights were 170,000, which is 53% of the pre-pandemic period.
The tourism industry has quickly recovered, and it has also driven the recovery of the accommodation, transportation, catering and shopping industries.
The Singapore government and tourism industry have high hopes for the Chinese market.
According to Ko Suk Tan, Assistant Director of the International Division of the Singapore Tourism Board, a survey by China Travel Platform showed that Singapore ranks among the top three in the world for safety, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular with Chinese tourists.
Before the COVID-19 pandemic, about 3.6 million Chinese people traveled to Singapore for vacation every year.
As China relaxes border policies, it is expected that the number of Chinese tourists will recover to 30 to 60 percent of the 2019 level this year.
Ko Shudan noted that the Tourism Bureau will closely monitor signals from China on further liberalization of outbound tourism policies to respond to changes in tourist demand.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms and travel agencies such as China Travel, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in response to the emerging trends in traveling abroad.
Looking ahead to the 2023 tourism market, the Singapore Tourism Board is confident that the recovery will be faster than expected, provided that there is no significant recession in the global economy that affects public revenues, and no new large-scale, deadly outbreaks.
A quick guide to Internet Finance
The book is shallow, and it can be said to be just an introduction, which can help you quickly understand the whole picture of Internet finance, but to understand it in depth, you still have to follow the diagram, and dig deep.
And given that it was published earlier, and the content about later on in the war between Alipay and WeChat Pay for subsidies has not been mentioned yet, the share of third-party payments now is unlikely to be as small as what is stated in the book.
Many people now have a mobile phone with them when they go out to eat, and businesses have abandoned the Union Bank because of the high bank charges.
But I am still very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book. It is obvious that the author has at least many years of financial observation experience, and is very familiar with the history of financial development at home and abroad. Therefore, it is possible to write such a simple and easy-to-understand content.
It is easy to write something high and wide, but it is difficult to write something simple and easy to understand.
